<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Calvin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1509">1509</a>–<a href="/years/1564">1564</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Jean Calvin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Calvin was a French theologian and reformer whose Institutes of the Christian Religion became a founding text of Reformed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a>. He arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> in 1536, was expelled in 1538 and returned in 1541, writing the church ordinances and working with the Consistory to discipline the city's morals. Geneva became a model and training centre for Reformed churches across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-calvin.jpg" alt="John Calvin" width="320" height="444" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous (France)Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_Calvin_Museum_Catharijneconvent_RMCC_s84_cropped.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His successor Heinrich Bullinger, head of the Zürich church from 1531 to 1575, corresponded across Europe, agreed the Consensus Tigurinus with <b>John Calvin</b> in 1549 and wrote the Second Helvetic Confession of 1566, adopted by Reformed churches from Hungary to Scotland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canton-of-zurich">Canton of Zürich · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Huldrych Zwingli began reform in Zürich from 1519, and the allied city of Geneva, where <b>John Calvin</b> worked from 1536 and founded his academy in 1559, became the centre of Reformed Protestantism, whose ideas spread to France, the Netherlands, Scotland, Hungary and the English-speaking world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/old-swiss-confederacy">Old Swiss Confederacy · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Reformers Martin Luther and <b>John Calvin</b> wrote works that defined Protestant theology, and the Council of Trent, from 1545 to 1563, defined Catholic teaching in response.</blockquote><a href="/religion/christianity">Christianity · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote><b>John Calvin</b>, whose Institutes of the Christian Religion first appeared in 1536, stressed the sovereignty of God, including his predestination of those to be saved, and a church governed by pastors and elders that supervised the morals of its members.</blockquote><a href="/movement/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>Martin Luther translated the New Testament into German from Erasmus’s Greek text, and Huldrych Zwingli, Melanchthon and <b>John Calvin</b> were humanists by training, as were many Catholic reformers.</blockquote><a href="/movement/renaissance-humanism">Renaissance humanism · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>The Dominican friar Girolamo Savonarola dominated Florence from 1494 to 1498; Anabaptists ruled Münster as a “New Jerusalem” until its prince-bishop retook the city in 1535; and historians still debate whether <b>John Calvin</b>’s Geneva, where a consistory of ministers and elders supervised morals from 1541, was a theocracy.</blockquote><a href="/government/theocracy">Theocracy · Through History</a></li></ul></section>
